      <!-- 3. Scientific Breakdown: Open Growth Plates vs Spinal Decompression (1-3 Inch Gain) -->
      <div class="fit-card p-6 md:p-8 rounded-2xl mb-12 bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700/80">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-black text-white mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>🧬</span> Epiphyseal Growth Plates vs. 1-3 Inch Postural Decompression
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          Understanding the difference between biological bone elongation and biomechanical spinal decompression:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-xs">
          <!-- Under 21 -->
          <div class="p-5 bg-slate-800/70 rounded-xl border border-teal-500/40 space-y-2">
            <span class="text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-teal-500/20 text-teal-400 border border-teal-500/30">
              Under Age 21: Biological Growth
            </span>
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-white">Open Epiphyseal Growth Plates</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              At the ends of long bones (femur, tibia, humerus), specialized bands of cartilage called <strong>epiphyseal growth plates</strong> actively divide. Under the stimulus of <strong>Human Growth Hormone (HGH), IGF-1, protein, calcium, and Vitamin D3</strong>, new cartilage calcifies into permanent bone tissue, lengthening your limbs.
            </p>
            <ul class="text-slate-400 space-y-1 list-disc pl-4 pt-1">
              <li>Open plates typically fuse between ages 16-18 (females) and 18-21 (males).</li>
              <li>High-impact jump drills (jump rope, sprinting) provide bone-mineralizing mechanical load.</li>
              <li>8.5 - 10 hours of sleep is critical (75% of HGH released in slow-wave deep sleep).</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <!-- Over 21 -->
          <div class="p-5 bg-slate-800/70 rounded-xl border border-emerald-500/40 space-y-2">
            <span class="text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 border border-emerald-500/30">
              At Any Age (21+): Biomechanical Growth
            </span>
            <h4 class="text-base font-bold text-white">1 to 3 Inches of Postural Height Gain</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Even after growth plates fuse, <strong>80% of adults lose 1 to 3 inches (2.5 to 7.5 cm)</strong> of vertical height to daily gravitational spinal compression, anterior pelvic tilt (swayback), rounded shoulders (kyphosis), and dehydrated intervertebral discs.
            </p>
            <ul class="text-slate-400 space-y-1 list-disc pl-4 pt-1">
              <li>The 23 intervertebral discs in your spine can expand by 1-2 mm each through traction (Dead Hangs).</li>
              <li>Fixing Anterior Pelvic Tilt (Pelvic Bridges) straightens the lumbar spine, gaining ~1 inch.</li>
              <li>Strengthening the posterior chain (Cobra stretch, Tadasana) eliminates slouching permanently.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

